          <h2>STAR answers to learn and adapt</h2>

          <div class="star-block">
            <span class="star-label">STAR 1</span>
            <h3>Research and evidence-led assessment redesign at URJC</h3>
            <p><strong>Question:</strong> Can you give an example of how you used research and evidence to redesign an assessment?</p>
            <p><strong>Situation:</strong> In my role as a lecturer at Universidad Rey Juan Carlos, I was responsible for redesigning the assessment model for Didactics of Social Sciences II. The previous approach relied too heavily on a final written exam and did not fully reflect the critical, reflective and practical competences the programme wanted students to develop. [file:156]</p>
            <p><strong>Task:</strong> My task was to create a more authentic and transparent assessment strategy aligned with the learning outcomes and informed by research on literacy, audiovisual literacy and citizenship education. [file:156][web:167]</p>
            <p><strong>Action:</strong> I reviewed the module outcomes and university regulations, then drew on current research and good practice in social sciences didactics and citizenship education. Based on that, I designed a continuous assessment model that combined a written test, a group project using heritage or audiovisual materials, a case study analysis and a written commentary on quality in education. I also wrote very explicit guidance for students, including weightings, minimum pass thresholds, re-evaluation rules, plagiarism expectations and writing quality requirements, and I refined the model through discussion with colleagues. [file:156][web:185]</p>
            <p><strong>Result:</strong> The result was a more coherent and authentic assessment model that gave students multiple ways to demonstrate what they knew and could do. It improved transparency, aligned more closely with the module aims and produced grade patterns that better matched the range of performance we observed. [file:156]</p>
          </div>

          <div class="star-block">
            <span class="star-label">STAR 2</span>
            <h3>TeacherAID and AI-related evidence-led design</h3>
            <p><strong>Question:</strong> Can you give an example of how you used research and evidence to design an assessment or instrument related to AI?</p>
            <p><strong>Situation:</strong> In the TeacherAID project, we needed an instrument that could capture teachers’ nuanced judgements about AI in education, rather than just collecting generic opinions or simple attitude scores. [file:154][file:155]</p>
            <p><strong>Task:</strong> My task was to help design a research and assessment instrument that was ethically sound, methodologically robust and practical for busy professionals, while producing rich and analysable data. [file:154][file:155]</p>
            <p><strong>Action:</strong> I reviewed literature on AI in education, teacher decision-making and attitude measurement, and used that evidence to help shape a multi-component instrument. It included a clear information and consent stage, factorial vignettes presenting realistic AI scenarios, and response formats such as sliders, ranking tasks and card sorting. We iteratively piloted and refined the wording, structure and scales to improve clarity, support analysis and minimise bias, while keeping alignment with ethical approval and good research practice. [file:154][file:155][web:158][web:185]</p>
            <p><strong>Result:</strong> The result was a richer and more robust instrument that captures how teachers weigh opportunities, risks and contextual factors around AI. For me, it is a strong example of combining theory, evidence, ethics and practical design to build an assessment instrument that is both rigorous and usable. [file:154][file:155]</p>
          </div>

          <div class="star-block">
            <span class="star-label">STAR 3</span>
            <h3>How my research profile shapes assessment design</h3>
            <p><strong>Question:</strong> How does your research profile influence your approach to assessment design?</p>
            <p><strong>Situation:</strong> Alongside my assessment and QA roles, I have developed a research profile documented in my ORCID record, including work on literacy, audiovisual literacy, social sciences didactics, citizenship education and AI-related conceptual frameworks. [web:167][web:177]</p>
            <p><strong>Task:</strong> My ongoing task has been to ensure that this research does not sit separately from practice, but actively informs how I design, review and quality-assure assessments across different settings. [web:167][web:177]</p>
            <p><strong>Action:</strong> In practice, this means I start from questions familiar in the research literature: what is being measured, how authentic the task is, what kinds of responses it is likely to elicit, and how different groups may experience the assessment. My work on audiovisual literacy and heritage encourages me to use multimodal, contextualised tasks, and my work on citizenship and AI pushes me to prioritise critical thinking, judgement and ethical reflection. I also keep up with research and regulatory discussions on validity, reliability, comparability and minimising bias, and I use those concepts explicitly when I evaluate or improve assessment strategies. [web:167][web:177][file:140][file:141][web:158][web:185]</p>
            <p><strong>Result:</strong> The result is that my assessment work is consistently evidence-informed. When I propose an assessment strategy or revision, it is grounded in a combination of practical experience, regulatory expectations and current research, rather than habit or intuition alone. [web:177][web:185]</p>
          </div>

          <div class="star-block">
            <span class="star-label">STAR 4</span>
            <h3>Regulation and compliance through QA roles</h3>
            <p><strong>Question:</strong> Describe a situation where you ensured assessments complied with regulations or organisational requirements.</p>
            <p><strong>Situation:</strong> In my external examiner and end-point assessment roles, I regularly worked with programme specifications, learning outcomes, assessment criteria, apprenticeship assessment plans and organisational guidance. [file:119][file:151]</p>
            <p><strong>Task:</strong> My task was to review whether assessment decisions, marking and evidence were aligned with those requirements and whether standards were being applied consistently and fairly. [file:119][file:151]</p>
            <p><strong>Action:</strong> I reviewed sampled work and decisions against the relevant frameworks, identified inconsistencies or risks, provided evidence-based written feedback and contributed to standardisation and action planning. I also checked my work against current regulatory expectations around validity, reliability, comparability, manageability and minimising bias, rather than relying on assumptions. [file:151][web:158][web:160]</p>
            <p><strong>Result:</strong> This supported clearer and more consistent decision-making, stronger documentation and more confidence that assessments were being delivered fairly and compliantly. [file:151]</p>
          </div>

          <div class="star-block">
            <span class="star-label">STAR 5</span>
            <h3>AI and authentic assessment</h3>
            <p><strong>Question:</strong> How has AI changed your thinking about assessment design?</p>
            <p><strong>Situation:</strong> As AI tools have become more widely available, purely theoretical written tasks have become more vulnerable to generic or tool-generated responses. [file:154][web:177]</p>
